## Commit Types

- `feat`: Adding new features, functionality that didn't exist before, or introducing new behavior
- `fix`: Addressing actual bugs that cause incorrect behavior in production code (e.g., fixing a known bug, correcting invalid or malfunctioning styles, resolving crashes or runtime errors)
- `refactor`: Structural improvements to the code without changing its behavior focusing on enhancing the internal structure, logic, or organization of the code without altering its external behavior
- `perf`: Changes that bring measurable performance improvements
- `style`: Cosmetic changes to code that do not affect its behavior (e.g., formatting codebase (e.g., via Prettier, ESLint), adjusting whitespace or indentation)
- `test`: Adding, modifying, fixing or improving tests (e.g., adding/updating unit, integration, or end-to-end tests, fixing broken test scripts, covering edge cases, adding QA scripts)
- `docs`: Changes to documentation, comments, or API descriptions (e.g., README.md, CHANGELOG.md)
- `build`: Changes affecting build-related components such as build tools, dependencies, project version, ...
- `ops`: Changes affecting operational aspects like infrastructure (IaC), deployment scripts, CI/CD pipelines, backups, monitoring, or recovery procedures, ...
- `chore`: Changes representing tasks like initial commit, modifying `.gitignore`, ...

## Commit Structure

```
<type>[optional scope]: <description>
<extended description>
```

- Type: Category of the change ((Commit Types)[#commit-types] section mentioned above)
- Scope: Optional noun describing the section of the codebase being changed (e.g., feat(auth), feat(api))
- Description: Concise summary of the code changes
- Extended Description: Bullet points including the changes done

### Handling Breaking Changes
If your commit introduces a breaking API change, it is clearly flagged by appending an exclamation mark (!) to the type, or by writing BREAKING CHANGE: in the footer of your commit message

## Examples

- `feat(api): add support for pagination in user endpoint`
- `fix(frontend): remove flickering effect on page refresh`
-
```
feat!: remove ticket list endpoint
refers to JIRA-1337
BREAKING CHANGE: ticket endpoints no longer supports list all entities.
```
- 
```
fix: add missing parameter to service call

The error occurred due to <reasons>.
```
- `build(release): bump version to 1.0.0`
- `refactor: extract utility functions for data validation`
- `test: cover edge cases for user registration validation`
- `build: upgrade webpack to version 5`
- `build(deps): update express to v4.18.1` ← (Dependency for production code)